WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.440
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:07.040
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.720
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.680
In other words,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.640
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.520
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.720
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.520
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.760
Today we explore
the possibilities

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.160
of how we might have control
over our own death—

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.040
retaining dignity, privacy,
and pain relief.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.800
And we start with a
close look at hospice.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.000
We have all sorts
of detailed stats about dying,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.360
but little about
the experience of death.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.480
For the minority who die under
the care of palliative care teams

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.240
one’s death could probably
be described as good,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.040
but there’s a suspicion that for
the majority who die in hospitals

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.680
or nursing homes
the experience is bad.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.000
And that’s where most people die.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.480
In spite of widespread preference
to die at home, in almost all populations,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.360
most deaths occur in institutions.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.800
And this doesn’t just have
consequences for the patient,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.880
but for the bereaved caregivers
as well.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.640
Patients with cancer who died
in an intensive care unit

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.120
or hospital experienced more
physical and emotional distress

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.680
and worse quality
of life at the end of life.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.960
And their caregivers
suffered five times the odds

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.080
of suffering
posttraumatic stress disorder,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.477
and nearly nine times the odds
of severe, prolonged disabling grief.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.400
When researchers have looked into
the care of dying patients in hospitals,

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.080
it hasn’t been pretty.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.160
Basic interventions to maintain
patients’ comfort were often not provided;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.360
contact with dying patients
was minimal;

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.840
and the distancing and isolation
worsened as death approached.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.640
For example, a 52-year-old woman with
metastatic cancer spread to her liver.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:01.000
She had gross abdominal distension,
was jaundiced and very breathless, but alert.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Her eyes were swollen,
and she shed yellow tears.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.240
The patient received no care
from the nurses delegated to give care.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.040
Yet in the nursing record
it was recorded

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.120
that attention had been given
to her personal hygiene,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.400
pressure areas to prevent bedsores,
oral hygiene, and eyes.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.040
But it was all a lie.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.360
The only attention she got was to receive
a commode from a nursing assistant.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:36.120
Contact time totaled six minutes over
the 4.5 hours; the researchers kept track.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.160
In contrast, what would
a good death look like?

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.480
Retaining control, dignity,
privacy, pain relief,

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.440
you choose where, you chose how,
emotional support,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.280
respecting your wishes,
saying goodbye,

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.840
being able to leave
when it’s time to go,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.680
and not have life prolonged
pointlessly.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.760
One’s best bet for all that
is access to hospice care.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.840
Palliative care is comfort measures
to relieve symptoms

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.240
and improve quality of life utilized
at any stage of a serious illness,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.360
whereas hospice is just
comfort measures,

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.160
when focus shifts
from curing the disease

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.000
to improving the quality
of one’s last days.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.600
Hospice is often framed
as “giving up,“ but ironically,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.760
when you compare hospice versus
non-hospice patient survival,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.840
the patients in hospice
actually live longer.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.320
Patients who choose hospice care live
on average about a month longer

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.560
than similar patients
who do not choose hospice.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.520
Randomize those with advanced
lung cancer to early palliative care,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:45.080
and they don’t live a month longer—
they live two and a half months longer!

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.360
That’s like the survival benefit
you might get

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.880
from a standard
chemotherapy regimen.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.720
And in fact, that’s one of the ways
hospice could extend survival

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.280
by avoiding the risk
of overtreatment with chemo

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.800
and its related toxicity.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.120
There are, however,
limits to palliative care.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.160
Even under hospice, where one would
assume excellent palliative care,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.880
there are those who appear
to unavoidably spend

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.960
their last months
in uncontrollable pain.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.600
And this unbearable suffering,
despite our best efforts,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.760
leads to requests for ending
a patient`s life prematurely.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.080
Although physician-assisted suicide
is gaining acceptance,

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.800
it’s only legal
in eight states plus D.C.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:35.440
In contrast, VSED
is legal throughout the U.S.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.080
Voluntarily Stopping Eating
and Drinking—VSED,

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.000
can be defined as a conscious
decision to voluntarily

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.520
and deliberately choose
to stop eating and drinking,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.240
with the primary intention
of hastening death

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.880
because of the persistence
of unacceptable suffering.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.280
I’ve talked about fasting
to extend one’s life;

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.840
what about fasting to shorten it?

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.000
We hear a lot about
physician-assisted suicide,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.160
but VSED has not gained
nearly as much attention.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.280
This is presented as perplexing,
since supposedly

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.520
it’s a relatively peaceful
and comfortable way to die.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.400
But is it really?
How long does it take?

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.960
How would you do it?

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:19.338
I’ll talk about the pros and cons
and practical implications next.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.800
Perhaps in an ideal world every patient
with a life-limiting illness

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.560
would receive optimal hospice
and palliative care

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.480
such that no one would ever wish
to hasten their own death.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.960
Unfortunately, the reality
is that despite our best efforts,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.520
some with terminal illness
continue to suffer,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.240
leading increasing numbers of patients
across the country to explore VSED

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.400
to escape intolerable suffering,

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.080
—Voluntarily Stopping Eating and Drinking.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.440
Some of the pros include dying at home,
which is what most people’s preference is.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.560
You don’t have to spend
your fleeting hours

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.480
dealing with doctors and lawyers.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.520
There is no waiting period.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.160
In Oregon, for example,
their Death with Dignity Act

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.560
requires a 15-day waiting period before
you can get physician assistance,

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.480
whereas choosing to stop eating and drinking
would seem to afford more control.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.080
Even just knowing it’s an option
can be a comfort.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:21.120
Those who learn about VSED
may feel empowered by the knowledge

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.160
that they can choose this option
without seeking anyone’s permission,

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.240
and because it’s legal
it can be openly discussed.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.400
Just knowing that there’s
“a way out’’

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.440
can provide relief from feelings
of desperation and entrapment.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.840
And the feeling of control
may itself be therapeutic.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.560
It can also prevent people from
contemplating a more violent way out,

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.520
and patients don’t have to feel pressured
to end their life prematurely

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.800
while they still can.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.040
Physician-assisted death with dignity laws

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.240
require patients to take
the lethal drug cocktail themselves.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.960
If you have ALS and worried about
losing your muscular function,

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.440
you might feel
the need to your end your life

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:02.800
before you’re ready
while you still have the capacity.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.040
In fact, Dr. Kevorkian’s first was a woman
diagnosed with Alzheimer’s

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.280
who wanted to end it
before it got too late,

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.040
potentially depriving
her and her family

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:15.760
of years she might
have still been able to enjoy.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.840
And while physician-assisted suicide entails
a single instantaneous and irrevocable act,

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.640
death from VSED occurs over several days,

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.720
allowing time for the patient
to change their mind

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.480
and for healing goodbyes.
But what is it actually like?

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:37.040
Ther's lots of anecdotes floating
around describing death from VSED

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.040
as peaceful, painless,
and dignified.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.760
Fortunately, the evidence
is more than just anecdotal.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.120
There have been several
independent studies;

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.840
so, let’s look at the data.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.920
Average time of death after stopping eating
and drinking was about seven days,

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.320
though 8% lasted more than two weeks.
And how was it?

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.200
Ask a hundred hospice nurses

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:06.440
and on a scale from 0 (a very bad death)
to 9 (a very good death),

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.600
the average score–the quality
of these deaths–

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.120
as rated by the nurses, was 8.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.920
The researchers concluded that
people usually die a “good” death

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.400
within two weeks after stopping
food and fluids,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:22.040
according to the nurses rating
those last days of life as peaceful,

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.840
with low levels
of pain and suffering.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.880
More so, even in those who chose
physician-assisted suicide.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.680
This was in Oregon, and so,
patients could have chosen that instead,

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:36.280
but nearly twice as many patients
decided instead

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:37.920
to take matters into their own hand

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.440
by voluntarily stopping eating, and drinking.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:46.440
Interestingly, loved ones seemed to agree,
with voluntary refusal of food and fluids

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.680
perceived as carrying less
"emotional baggage" for the family,

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.680
more letting go than taking
a more active approach.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.480
A study in the Netherlands
that interviewed confidants,

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.760
like friends and family
that witnessed it,

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:03.240
found that most considered it
a “dignified death.”

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:07.640
Hospice physicians seem to agree,
with nearly 90 percent surveyed reporting

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.720
that their VSED patients experienced
peaceful and comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.480
deaths.
&nbsp;

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.880
The state of terminal dehydration
may even have some analgesic effect,

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:22.040
some pain-killing effects, presumed
to be due to the release of endorphins,

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:23.960
which act as natural pain blockers.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:30.720
So, concluded this review, VSED may reflect
all 12 principles of a ‘good death’,

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.720
retaining control, dignity...

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.680
One of the most famous accounts

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.480
was Dr. David Eddy’s description
of his own mother’s VSED:

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.120
“My mother was elated.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.600
The next day happened
to be her 85th birthday,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.200
which we celebrated with a party,
balloons and all.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.280
She was beaming from ear to ear.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.480
She had done it.
She had found the way.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:59.440
She relished her last piece of chocolate,
and then stopped eating and drinking. &nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:02.640
The last four days,
my mother greeted her visitors

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.360
with the first smiles
she had shown for months.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.940
She energetically reminisced
about the great times she had had

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.840
and about things she was proud of.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.640
She especially hoped I would tell
you about her traveling alone

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:15.400
across Africa at the age of 70,

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.800
and surviving a capsized raft
on Wyoming's Snake River at 82.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.720
She also found
a calming self-acceptance

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:25.440
in describing things
of which she was not proud.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.160
She slept between visits but woke up
brightly whenever she was touched

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.000
to share more memories and to say
a few more things she wanted us to know.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.880
On the fifth day it was
more difficult to wake her.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:41.320
When we would take her hand
she would open her eyes and smile,

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:44.440
but she was too drowsy
and weak to talk very much.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.640
On the sixth day,
we could not wake her.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:50.000
Her face was relaxed
in her natural smile.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.840
She was breathing unevenly,
but peacefully.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.280
We held her hands for another two hours,
until she died."

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:00.200
"Without hoarding pills,
without making me into a criminal,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.000
without putting
a bag over her head,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:05.600
and without huddling in a van
with a carbon monoxide machine,

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.840
she had found a way to bring
her life gracefully to a close.”

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:11.160
“Write about this, David.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.680
Tell others how well
this worked for me.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.040
I’d like this to be my gift.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.640
Whether they are terminally ill,
in intractable pain,

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:23.360
or, like me, just knowing that
the right time has come for them,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.360
more people might want to know
that this way exists.

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:30.583
And maybe more physicians
will help them find it.”

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.880
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:37.960
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.120
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.800
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.720
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.880
go to the Nutrition Facts
